# Expectation record for 05_pointers/004_multilevel.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_multilevel
area               = 05_pointers
description        = Multilevel indirection and pointer-to-pointer modification, folded and volatile-runtime variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Every pointer at every level designates a live object for as long as it is used. The
second- and third-level pointers point at pointer objects that are in scope, and the block
that introduces the nested pointer pair uses the third-level pointer only inside that
block, so no pointer to an object with automatic storage duration outlives its object: on
leaving the block the third-level pointer is assigned before it is next read, never read
while stale. All table indices are within pslots' four elements, and the two one-past-end
pointers the span lines build -- pslots + 4 and &(*parr)[4] -- are subtracted only and are
never dereferenced. The table and slots[3] are explicitly restored before the runtime
section so its starting state is stated rather than assumed.
Writes go only to objects that are not const.
No signed integer overflow, no shift out of range, no aliasing violation, no read of
uninitialized storage, no object modified twice between sequence points, at most one
side-effecting argument per call, and no dependence on padding bytes or on the relative
addresses of unrelated objects. The only external symbol is printf; no header is included,
no file, socket, environment variable, clock or random source is touched, and every input
is a literal in this source file.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
No address or pointer value is printed. Pointer facts appear only as differences,
comparisons and alignment residues, and every pointer difference is cast to long long
before printing, so the printed text is identical whether ptrdiff_t is 32 or 64 bits.
Plain char is never printed in a signedness-dependent way. The only conversion
specifiers used anywhere in this program are %d and %lld, both of which are
target-width independent.
END
